def z(n,key,new,why,s,option=None):qe(q,'virt-isolation-security-q'+str(n),key,new,why,s,option)
e('Neither stops it doing something dangerous. A container\'s root user, left alone, could still load a kernel module, remount `/sys` or reboot the host.','These mechanisms provide real restrictions, but are not the whole security policy. Powerful actions depend on capability scope, namespaces and other checks; UID 0 in a child user namespace cannot simply load host modules or reboot the host.','Originalrootidentityignoresnamespacecredentials/rebootsemantics.',[us,cap])
e('Every container on a host shares **one kernel**. That kernel exposes more than 300 system calls and is tens of millions of lines of code, and one bug in it can let a process escape.','Native Linux process containers share a host kernel. Its attack surface depends on kernel version, architecture and enabled interfaces. A reachable vulnerability can compromise that boundary; VM-backed runtimes use a different boundary.','Removeunqualifiedkernelcode/syscallcountandallcontaineridentity.',[g])
e('  weaker, cheaper','  possible defence layers','No universal totalorderingsecuritycost.',[g])
e('  v  stronger, costlier','  v  assess threat model and workload','No universal stronger/costlierguarantee.',[g])
e('**Bounding** caps what can ever be gained, even by running a setuid binary. A runtime drops capabilities from the bounding set before `execve`, so the container can never get them back.','**Bounding** limits file-permitted capability gains on exec and additions to the inheritable set; it is not an absolute mask on all current or inheritable capability paths. Runtimes must configure permitted, effective, inheritable, ambient and bounding sets consistently. New user namespaces also have their own capability scope.','Boundingalonecannotguaranteeneverregain;inheritableexception.',[cap,us])
e('By default, Docker keeps 14 capabilities,','The Moby default list retrieved for this review contains 14 capabilities,','Time/platform/configqualified.', ['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/moby/moby/master/daemon/pkg/oci/caps/defaults.go'])
e('A service that binds port 80 needs `NET_BIND_SERVICE`, or it can just listen on 8080.','Binding port 80 needs NET_BIND_SERVICE only when that port is below the network namespace\'s ip_unprivileged_port_start threshold. Some runtimes lower the threshold; check it before adding a capability.','Lowportprivilegeconfigurable.',[ip])
e('`prctl(PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS)` turns that off','`prctl(PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, 1, 0, 0, 0)` prevents exec from granting new privileges','CorrectAPIandscope;notallfutureprivilegedoperationsdisabled.',[sc])
e('seccomp_rule_add(ctx,\n  SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM),\n  SCMP_SYS(keyctl), 0);\nseccomp_rule_add(ctx,\n  SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM),\n  SCMP_SYS(kexec_load), 0);\nseccomp_load(ctx);   /* irreversible */','if (!ctx) fail("seccomp_init");\nif (seccomp_rule_add(ctx,\n      SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM),\n      SCMP_SYS(keyctl), 0) < 0)\n  fail("keyctl rule");\nif (seccomp_rule_add(ctx,\n      SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM),\n      SCMP_SYS(kexec_load), 0) < 0)\n  fail("kexec rule");\nif (seccomp_load(ctx) < 0)\n  fail("seccomp_load");\nseccomp_release(ctx); /* filter remains */','UncheckedsecurityAPIcouldsilentlyleaveunfiltered.', ['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/seccomp_rule_add.3.html'])
e('A minimal filter using libseccomp in C:','A libseccomp C fragment (supply errno/seccomp headers, link libseccomp and a fail helper that exits; successful load applies to the calling thread unless synchronisation is requested):','Statecontextandthreads.',[sc])
e('In effect it blocks around 44 of the 300-plus syscalls, including `kexec_load`, `init_module`, `reboot`, `keyctl`, `add_key`, `bpf` and, without `CAP_SYS_ADMIN`, `mount` and namespace-creating `unshare`/`clone` flags.','The exact rules depend on runtime version, architecture and capabilities. The retrieved Moby profile restricts calls such as keyctl and namespace operations, with conditional allowances for some privileged operations. Inspect the deployed profile rather than assuming a fixed count or universal deny list.','44countnotstable;capconditionalbpf/reboot/moduleoperations.',[dv])
e('with the strictest result winning.','with action precedence deciding the result (and defined rules for equal-precedence data). A filter applies per thread unless explicitly synchronised to other threads.','Strictestcolloquialnotsufficientthreadscope.',[sc])
e('Filters must also check the architecture field. Otherwise a process can make 32-bit (`int 0x80`) or x32 syscalls, whose numbers differ, and slip past rules written for x86-64.','Filters must identify the syscall ABI. Checking arch distinguishes i386 from x86-64, but x32 uses AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64 too and marks syscall numbers with __X32_SYSCALL_BIT; arch alone is insufficient for that case.','Criticalx32bypasssamearch.',[sc])
e('CVE-2022-0185, a heap overflow in the file system context code, required `unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER)` to get `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` inside a new user namespace. Docker\'s default profile blocked that. Kubernetes, which historically ran pods *unconfined*, did not, unless the pod set `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`.','CVE-2022-0185 affected filesystem-context handling on vulnerable kernels. An unprivileged attacker could reach the required namespaced CAP_SYS_ADMIN through user namespaces where permitted; already-privileged callers did not need that exact route. A profile blocking the necessary calls can close this path. Kubernetes seccomp defaults and policy must be checked on the actual cluster.','Unshare notstrictlyrequiredforallattackers;configurationdependent exposure.',['https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/cve-2022-0185',kr,dv])
e('- **SELinux** (RHEL, Fedora) labels every process and file. Container processes run as `container_t`, and each container gets a unique MCS category pair such as `s0:c123,c456`. If a container escapes its mount namespace, it still cannot read another container\'s files, because the categories don\'t match.','- **SELinux** policies use labels such as container_t and MCS categories to constrain permitted access. Actual domains, shared labels, categories and enforcement depend on runtime/policy configuration; containers do not invariably receive distinct categories.','Labelsharing/privilegedpolicyexceptions.',['https://github.com/containers/container-selinux/blob/main/container.te'])
e('The LSM is what stops a process that has broken out of its mount namespace from reading `/etc/shadow`.','An enforcing LSM policy can deny file access even when a pathname becomes reachable. DAC permissions and credentials also matter; merely enabling an LSM does not prove a particular file is denied.','Notguaranteedspecificpolicyorsolemechanism.',['https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/apparmor/',cap])
e('The process has full capabilities *inside its namespaces*, but to the host kernel it is UID 100000. If it escapes, it is an unprivileged user.','New user namespaces initially grant capabilities scoped to that user namespace, but runtimes may drop them. Container UID 0 in this example maps to host UID 100000. A mount-visibility escape alone does not grant host-root credentials; a kernel exploit can defeat more than mount isolation.','Rootnsdoesnotmitigatekernelescalationguaranteed.',[us])
e('A kernel bug in an allowed path still breaks everything. Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) let any process overwrite read-only page-cache pages using plain `splice` and `write`, both of which any profile must allow. So it worked from inside a default container.','A reachable kernel flaw can bypass important protections. Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) enabled constrained modification of readable file-backed page-cache data on vulnerable kernels, including read-only files; exploitability depended on reachable files and calls. No profile is required to allow splice, and the flaw did not mean every process could overwrite arbitrary host files.','Arbitrarypageoverwrite/mandatorysplice/allcontainerescape false.',['https://dirtypipe.cm4all.com/'])
e('then gain root on the host the next time `runc` ran.','leading to host code execution under the vulnerable runtime\'s privileges. This is a historical vulnerability, not a claim about patched current releases.','Scopehistoricalruntimeattack.',['https://kubernetes.io/blog/2019/02/11/runc-and-cve-2019-5736/'])
e('An attacker must first compromise the Sentry and then attack the host through that narrow interface.','This design reduces direct host-kernel exposure and adds a sandbox boundary; it is not a proof that every possible escape needs the same two-step exploit chain.','Notuniversaltwostepescapeproof.',[g])
e('CPU-bound code runs at near-native speed.','CPU-bound application instructions execute natively, so workloads with few system interactions may have lower overhead; measure the actual workload and platform.','No universalnear-nativeperformance.',[gp])
e('The minimalism pays off. The project claims boot in under 125 ms, under 5 MiB of memory overhead per microVM, and up to 150 microVMs created per second per host. Each guest has its own kernel, so a guest kernel exploit only gets the attacker into a VM that is still behind hardware virtualisation. AWS Lambda and Fargate run on it.','Firecracker publishes configuration-specific targets: at most 125 ms from InstanceStart to guest /sbin/init and at most 5 MiB VMM overhead under its documented small-VM, tuned-kernel host conditions. These exclude guest RAM and have workload/configuration caveats; they are not universal end-to-end launch figures or capacity guarantees. The project documents use in Lambda and Fargate. A guest kernel remains behind a virtualisation boundary.','Scopeprimaryperformancefiguresandomituniversal150/sec.',[fs,fc])
e('Escaping the guest gives you an unprivileged, filtered process.','The jailer limits the VMM\'s host privileges; the outcome of any escape depends on the vulnerability and deployment. A guest-kernel compromise alone is not the same as executing host VMM code.','Distinguishguestkernel/VMMescapeandnotguaranteedendpoint.',[fj])
e('| runc + defaults | Shared kernel, filtered | ~0 |','| runc + defaults | Shared kernel, filtered | Workload dependent |','Notzerooverhead.',[g])
e('| + rootless/userns | Unprivileged on host | Small |','| + rootless/userns | Scoped host identity | Configuration dependent |','No universaloverhead.',[us])
e('| Firecracker/Kata | Separate kernel + VT-x | ~MBs RAM, ~100 ms |','| Firecracker/Kata | Guest kernel + hardware support | Guest/VMM resources |','ARMexistsandno universalRAM/bootfigures.',[fc])
e('**`--privileged`** grants all capabilities, disables seccomp and the LSM profile and exposes every host device.','**`--privileged`**, in ordinary rootful Docker, grants broad capabilities/device access and relaxes normal seccomp and LSM restrictions. Rootless/namespace scope and host configuration still matter.','Rootfulscope.',[dc])
e('**Host mounts.** `hostPath: /`, the Docker socket or `/proc` from the host bypass every layer above.','**Host mounts.** Sensitive host paths or daemon sockets can undermine isolation by exposing data or authority. They do not literally disable every remaining credential, seccomp or LSM check.','Alllayersbypassfalse.',[g])
e('Docker\'s default allowlist blocks around 44 risky calls.','deployed allowlist contents vary with version and capabilities.','Removeunstablecountsummary.',[dv])
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Verification limits\n> The historical vulnerabilities are source-checked examples, not exploits run during this review. No live gVisor, Firecracker or Kata isolation/performance experiment was performed. Published Firecracker targets are scoped above; no universal sandbox ranking, escape guarantee or launch-rate estimate is supported here.\n\n## Further reading','Visiblegaps.',[g,fs])
z(1,'text','Native runc containers use the host kernel, while a VM guest kernel is separated from host resources by the VMM/hardware boundary','Kernelbugnotautomaticallycompromiseseverything.',[g],1)
z(1,'workedExample','Native container syscalls enter the shared kernel. A sufficiently powerful reachable flaw can break isolation, but not every bug grants arbitrary kernel execution. VM applications call a guest kernel; compromising it does not by itself cross the VMM boundary. Shared folders, passthrough and configuration can add paths that must also be assessed.','DirtyPipenotgeneralkernelcodeexecution.',[g])
